// Experimental WASI mode demo: Net.get via wasi:http (Phase 1)
// (capa --wasm --component --wasi).
//
// Migrates Net.get to the canonical WASI Preview 2 outbound HTTP chain:
//
//   net.get(url) -> fields.new()
//                -> outgoing-request.new(fields)
//                -> set-method(GET) / set-scheme / set-authority /
//                   set-path-with-query   (resolved from the LITERAL url
//                                          at compile time)
//                -> outgoing-handler.handle(request)        [wasi:http]
//                -> future-incoming-response.subscribe + pollable.block
//                   (synchronous wait)                      [wasi:io/poll]
//                -> future-incoming-response.get  (the triple-result lift:
//                   option<result<result<incoming-response, error-code>>>)
//                -> incoming-response.status  (status >= 400 -> Err,
//                   PARITY with the urllib oracle which raises HTTPError)
//                -> incoming-response.consume -> incoming-body.stream
//                -> input-stream.blocking-read LOOP, accumulating chunks
//                   until stream-error::closed (EOF)        [wasi:io/streams]
//                -> Ok(String) (the UTF-8-decoded body)
//                -> every OWN resource handle (outgoing-request /
//                   outgoing-body / future / pollable / incoming-response /
//                   incoming-body / input-stream, and any carried error)
//                   is dropped on every exit path.
//
// The Ok(String) body is byte-identical to the Python backend's
// urlopen(url).read().decode("utf-8") and to the capa:host bridge.
// A status >= 400 or a transport failure is an Err(IoError). Stdio stays
// on capa:host -- the hybrid coexistence this mode validates.
// See docs/design/wasi_mode.md.
//
// THE NET CEILING IS GUEST-SIDE (the honest asymmetry). Unlike the Fs
// preopen ceiling and the Env env-set ceiling -- both Level 1, imposed by
// the WASI host at instantiate -- wasmtime's wasi:http C-API is ALLOW-ALL
// (no allowed-hosts surface in this release), so there is no host ceiling
// to map onto. The compiler instead collects the HOSTS of the LITERAL
// urls passed to net.get and the guest wrapper refuses any host the
// program never names (codegen-enforced). A DYNAMIC url (built at runtime)
// is fail-closed: it cannot be split into a wasi:http request at compile
// time, so the call returns Err WITHOUT reaching the network. A program
// that needs dynamic Net hosts runs unchanged on the default capa:host
// backend (drop --wasi). Net.post / restrict_to / allows are NOT migrated
// in Phase 1; they are rejected at compile time under --wasi.
//
// Run against a LOCAL server (no external network) -- e.g. start a tiny
// HTTP server on 127.0.0.1:8080 returning a known body, then:
//
//   capa --wasm --component --wasi --run examples/wasm/wasi_net_get.capa
//
// (Adjust the literal url below to the host:port your server listens on.)

fun main(net: Net, stdio: Stdio)
    let r = net.get("http://127.0.0.1:8080/hello")
    match r
        Ok(body) -> stdio.println("get ok, body:\n${body}")
        Err(e) -> stdio.println("get failed: ${e}")
    // A host the program never names as a literal would be denied by the
    // guest-side ceiling; here every net.get url is a literal, so the
    // ceiling is exactly { 127.0.0.1 }.
